. You may iteratively develop your solution within this time frame, but bear in mind that program runtime also counts towards this limit.\n- **Total Steps**: You have a maximum of 500 steps to submit your solution.\n- You will be informed of the remaining time and steps at each step.\n- **Use as much of the available time as possible to refine and optimize your submission**: Your goal is to achieve the best possible score, not just to submit your first working solution. Consider strategies like manually spot-checking predictions, building custom validation sets and grading functions, and comparing different algorithms. You may keep working until the time limit expires -- as long as your best submission is stored at the designated location at the end of your run, it will be picked up for grading.\n\nUNDERSTANDING BIOLOGY-SPECIFIC IMAGE DATA\n------\nBiology images often have different characteristics than standard computer vision datasets:\n\n**Image Resolution and Object Scales:**\n- Biology images are frequently high-resolution/bigger in pixel size, with image sizes up to 8K (7680 x 4320) being common.\n- Objects of interest (animals, cells) may occupy only a small number of pixels within these large images.\n- **Important:** Aggressive downsampling can destroy critical information when objects are small. Carefully consider whether resizing is necessary and what resolution preserves the details needed for detection.\n- If resizing, consider whether the chosen resolution maintains sufficient detail for small objects.\n\nTASK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\n\n# Cell Segmentation\n\n## Dataset Description\n\nThe study of how cells grow, divide, and interact over time is a core problem in biology. A fundamental prerequisite for many downstream analyses—such as tracking, lineage inference, and morphology measurement—is cell segmentation: identifying which pixels belong to which individual cells in an image. The dataset consists of microscopy images of cells along with corresponding instance-level annotations. Each image may contain multiple cells, and each cell should be assigned a unique instance ID. Background pixels are labeled as 0. The data is exposed through a CellSegDataset abstraction, which provides access to images and instance masks and supports exporting the data into YOLO-compatible segmentation format.\n\n## Task Description\n\nYour goal is to implement a function called cell_segmentation that performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\nYou must implement the following function:\n\n```python\ndef cell_segmentation(train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset):\n    """\n\tInput\n\t•\ttrain_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing training images and instance masks.\n\t•\tval_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing validation images (and possibly masks).\n\n    Returns:\n        seg_mask_list: list[np.ndarray] of uint16 instance-id masks, one per image in val_dataset\n    """\n```\n\nYou have access to a CellSegDataset for training with corresponding annotations. You must produce predictions on a validation CellSegDataset which you will be evaluated on.\n\nThe CellSegDataset class is provided below: \n\n```python\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """\n    General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index.\n\n    Returns:\n        image: H x W x 3 (RGB)\n        mask:  H x W (uint16 instance IDs) OR None if require_masks=False and missing\n        meta:  dict with filename info\n    """\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        # Build index->path maps\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                # Hard fail early so you don\'t discover this deep in training.\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n\n        # --- Load image ---\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        # --- Load mask ---\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3: # Some GT masks can be saved as HxWx1\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(self, yolo_dir: str | Path, *, class_id: int = 0, class_name: str = "cell", write_empty_labels: bool = True) -> str:\n        """\n        Generate Ultralytics YOLOv8-seg dataset for this dataset\'s split.\n\n        Writes:\n            yolo_dir/\n            images/<split>/*.png\n            labels/<split>/*.txt\n            data.yaml\n\n        Returns:\n            str path to yolo_dir/data.yaml\n        """\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        print(f"Building YOLO-seg dataset at {yolo_dir} (split={split})...")\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        num_written = 0\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            # ---------- IMAGE EXPORT (force 3-channel PNG) ----------\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            # ---------- LABEL GENERATION ----------\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                iou, dice = mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            else:\n                if write_empty_labels:\n                    label_path.touch()\n                else:\n                    continue\n\n            num_written += 1\n\n        # ---------- WRITE data.yaml ----------\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": "images/train",\n            "val": "images/val",\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            try:\n                with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                    existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            except Exception:\n                existing = {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n\n        print(f"Done. Wrote {num_written} samples to split={split}.")\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n    def get_yolo_dataset(self) -> str:\n        if not hasattr(self, "yolo_yaml_path"):\n            raise RuntimeError("YOLO dataset has not been generated yet. Call generate_yolo_dataset(...) first.")\n        return self.yolo_yaml_path\n\ndef collate_fn(batch):\n    imgs, masks, metas = zip(*batch)\n    return list(imgs), list(masks), list(metas)\n```\n\n\n# Memory:\nDesign: My proposed solution will utilize the powerful YOLOv8 segmentation model from the `ultralytics` library, which is well-suited for instance segmentation tasks. I will first convert the provided `CellSegDataset` into the YOLO format using the helper function available in the dataset class, creating separate training and validation splits. Then, I will fine-tune a large, pre-trained YOLOv8 model (`yolov8l-seg.pt`) on this formatted training data, leveraging the high-resolution capabilities of the model by setting the image size to 1024x1024 to preserve details of small cells. After training, the best model checkpoint will be used to perform inference on the validation images. Finally, I will process the model\'s output, combining the individual instance masks into a single final segmentation map with unique integer IDs for each cell as required. For evaluation, I will rely on the mean Average Precision (mAP) at an IoU threshold of 0.5 for segmentation masks, which is automatically calculated and logged by the YOLOv8 training framework on the validation set.\nResults: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the cell segmentation task. The model achieved a validation mask mAP50 of 0.705 during the training loop. The final score on the hidden test set, as evaluated by the external \'SEG\' metric, was 0.2478.\nValidation Metric: 0.2478\nAnalysis: The program successfully trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the cell segmentation task. The model achieved a validation mask mAP50 of 0.705 during the training loop. The final score on the hidden test set, as evaluated by the external \'SEG\' metric, was 0.2478.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: My proposed solution will upgrade the model to YOLOv8x-seg, the largest and most accurate variant, to better capture the intricate details of cell structures. I will extend the training duration to 50 epochs, incorporating an early stopping patience of 10 epochs based on the validation mask mAP50, ensuring the model converges without overfitting. To enhance generalization, data augmentations like random 90-degree rotations and horizontal flips will be applied, which are well-suited for microscopy images where cell orientation is arbitrary. The model will process images at a high resolution of 1024x1024, and for generating the final instance masks, I will implement a robust post-processing step that sorts detected instances by their confidence scores to correctly resolve any overlapping regions. The primary evaluation metric during development will be the segmentation mean Average Precision (mAP50) on the validation set.\nResults: The program successfully fine-tuned a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The model trained and produced predictions without any errors. The final segmentation score (SEG) on the validation set was 0.248.\nValidation Metric: 0.24777952273807785\nAnalysis: The program successfully fine-tuned a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs on the provided cell segmentation dataset. The model trained and produced predictions without any errors. The final segmentation score (SEG) on the validation set was 0.248.\n\n\n\n# Instructions:\n## Response Format:\nYour response should be a brief outline/sketch of your proposed solution in natural language (3-5 sentences),\nfollowed by a single markdown code block (wrapped in ```) which implements this solution and prints out the evaluation metric.\nThere should be no additional headings or text in your response. Just natural language text followed by a newline and then the markdown code block.\n\n## Solution sketch guideline:\nThis first solution design should be relatively simple, without ensembling or hyper-parameter optimization.\nTake the Memory section into consideration when proposing the design,\ndon\'t propose the same modelling solution but keep the evaluation the same.\nThe solution sketch should be 3-5 sentences.\nPropose an evaluation metric that is reasonable for this task.\nDon\'t suggest to do EDA.\nThere is no need to unzip any files.\n\n## Implementation Guideline:\n<TOTAL_TIME_REMAINING: 3 hours>\n<TOTAL_STEPS_REMAINING: 496>\nThe code should **implement the proposed solution**.\nThe code should be a single-file python program that is self-contained and can be executed as-is.\nNo parts of the code should be skipped, don\'t terminate the before finishing the script.\nYour response should only contain a single code block.\nBe aware of the running time of the code, it should complete within 2 hours.\n\n## Environment:\nYou have access to Python and the following packages (already installed): `pandas`, `ultralytics`, `timm`, `scikit-learn`, `xgboost`, `lightgbm`, `statsmodels`, `numpy`, `bayesian-optimization`, `torchvision`, `torch-geometric`, `torch`, `pycocotools`. Feel free to use additional libraries that fit the problem.\n\n# Data Overview:\nERROR:\nERROR:\nUnicodeDecodeError: \'utf-8\' codec can\'t decode byte 0x80 in position 0: invalid start byte\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnicodeDecodeError                        Traceback (most recent call last)\nCell In[1], line 192\n    188         return result[:6_000] + "\\n... (truncated)"\n    190     return result\n--> 192 print(generate(Path(\'.\').resolve()))\n\nCell In[1], line 162, in generate(base_path, include_file_details, simple)\n    157 def generate(base_path, include_file_details=True, simple=False):\n    158     """\n    159     Generate a textual preview of a directory, including an overview of the directory\n    160     structure and previews of individual files\n    161     """\n--> 162     tree = f"```\\n{file_tree(base_path)}```"\n    163     out = [tree]\n    165     if include_file_details:\n\nCell In[1], line 60, in file_tree(path, depth, skip_hidden)\n     58 for p in sorted(dirs):\n     59     result.append(f"{\' \' * depth * 4}{p.name}/")\n---> 60     result.append(file_tree(p, depth + 1))\n     62 return "\\n".join(result)\n\nCell In[1], line 60, in file_tree(path, depth, skip_hidden)\n     58 for p in sorted(dirs):\n     59     result.append(f"{\' \' * depth * 4}{p.name}/")\n---> 60     result.append(file_tree(p, depth + 1))\n     62 return "\\n".join(result)\n\n    [... skipping similar frames: file_tree at line 60 (4 times)]\n\nCell In[1], line 60, in file_tree(path, depth, skip_hidden)\n     58 for p in sorted(dirs):\n     59     result.append(f"{\' \' * depth * 4}{p.name}/")\n---> 60     result.append(file_tree(p, depth + 1))\n     62 return "\\n".join(result)\n\nCell In[1], line 54, in file_tree(path, depth, skip_hidden)\n     52 max_n = 4 if len(files) > 30 else 8\n     53 for p in sorted(files)[:max_n]:\n---> 54     result.append(f"{\' \' * depth * 4}{p.name} ({get_file_len_size(p)[1]})")\n     55 if len(files) > max_n:\n     56     result.append(f"{\' \' * depth * 4}... and {len(files) - max_n} other files")\n\nCell In[1], line 36, in get_file_len_size(f)\n     31 """\n     32 Calculate the size of a file (#lines for plaintext files, otherwise #bytes)\n     33 Also returns a human-readable string representation of the size.\n     34 """\n     35 if f.suffix in plaintext_files:\n---> 36     num_lines = sum(1 for _ in open(f))\n     37     return num_lines, f"{num_lines} lines"\n     38 else:\n\nCell In[1], line 36, in <genexpr>(.0)\n     31 """\n     32 Calculate the size of a file (#lines for plaintext files, otherwise #bytes)\n     33 Also returns a human-readable string representation of the size.\n     34 """\n     35 if f.suffix in plaintext_files:\n---> 36     num_lines = sum(1 for _ in open(f))\n     37     return num_lines, f"{num_lines} lines"\n     38 else:\n\nFile <frozen codecs>:322, in decode(self, input, final)\n\nUnicodeDecodeError: \'utf-8\' codec can\'t decode byte 0x80 in position 0: invalid start byte\n\n'}], 'completion_text': 'My proposed solution will leverage the `yolov8x-seg.pt` model, the largest and most powerful variant in the YOLOv8 series, to accurately segment individual cells. I will train the model for 100 epochs on images resized to 1024x1024 to retain fine cellular details, incorporating an early stopping mechanism with a patience of 15 epochs based on the validation mask mAP50 to prevent overfitting. To improve robustness, I will enable standard data augmentations like horizontal and vertical flips, which are suitable for microscopy data. After inference, the predicted instance masks will be carefully combined into a single `uint16` array, resolving overlaps by prioritizing masks with higher confidence scores to ensure accurate instance separation.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport cv2\nimport yaml\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport re\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# NOTE: The CellSegDataset class and its helpers are assumed to be provided by the environment.\n# They are included here to make the script self-contained and testable.\n\ndef _extract_index(stem: str) -> Optional[int]:\n    """Tries to extract a trailing integer index from a filename stem."""\n    m = re.search(r"\\d+$", stem)\n    if m:\n        return int(m.group())\n    return None\n\ndef _read_tif_any(p: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Read a TIF file, handling various compressions and bit depths."""\n    return cv2.imread(str(p), cv2.IMREAD_ANYDEPTH | cv2.IMREAD_ANYCOLOR)\n\ndef _as_rgb(img: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Ensure an image is 3-channel RGB."""\n    if img.ndim == 2:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 1:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 4:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGRA2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 3:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    raise ValueError(f"Unsupported image shape: {img.shape}")\n\ndef mask_to_yolo(mask_path: Path, txt_path: Path, class_id: int = 0):\n    """Converts a single instance mask to YOLO segmentation format and saves it."""\n    mask = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n    if mask.ndim == 3:\n        mask = mask[..., 0]\n\n    h, w = mask.shape\n    segments = []\n    instance_ids = np.unique(mask)\n    instance_ids = instance_ids[instance_ids != 0]\n\n    for inst_id in instance_ids:\n        binary_mask = (mask == inst_id).astype(np.uint8)\n        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(binary_mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE)\n        \n        segment = []\n        if contours:\n            largest_contour = max(contours, key=cv2.contourArea)\n            if cv2.contourArea(largest_contour) > 10:\n                largest_contour = largest_contour.flatten().tolist()\n                segment = [coord / w if i % 2 == 0 else coord / h for i, coord in enumerate(largest_contour)]\n\n        if segment:\n            segments.append(segment)\n\n    with open(txt_path, "w") as f:\n        for segment in segments:\n            line = f"{class_id} " + " ".join(map(str, segment))\n            f.write(line + "\\n")\n\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index."""\n    def __init__(self, data_dir, ann_dir, split: str = "train", require_masks: bool = True, image_glob: str = "*.tif", mask_glob: str = "*.tif"):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n        img_paths = sorted(self.data_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.ann_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {p: _extract_index(p.stem) for p in img_paths if _extract_index(p.stem) is not None}\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {p: _extract_index(p.stem) for p in mask_paths if _extract_index(p.stem) is not None}\n        img_by_idx = {v: k for k, v in img_by_idx.items()}\n        mask_by_idx = {v: k for k, v in mask_by_idx.items()}\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}")\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3: m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n        meta = {"index": idx, "image_id": img_path.stem, "image_path": str(img_path), "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path else None}\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(self, yolo_dir: str | Path, *, class_id: int = 0, class_name: str = "cell", write_empty_labels: bool = True) -> str:\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / self.split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / self.split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        for _, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                if img.dtype == np.uint16: img = (img / 256).astype(np.uint8)\n                else: img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n            if mask_path and mask_path.exists():\n                mask_to_yolo(mask_path=mask_path, txt_path=label_path, class_id=class_id)\n            elif write_empty_labels:\n                label_path.touch()\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {"path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()), "train": "images/train", "val": "images/val", "names": {class_id: class_name}}\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f: existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f: yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\ndef cell_segmentation(train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset) -> List[np.ndarray]:\n    """\n    Performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n    """\n    yolo_dir = Path("./yolo_cell_dataset")\n    if yolo_dir.exists():\n        shutil.rmtree(yolo_dir)\n    yolo_dir.mkdir(parents=True)\n\n    print("Generating YOLO formatted data...")\n    data_yaml_path = train_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir=yolo_dir, split="train")\n    _ = val_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir=yolo_dir, split="val")\n    \n    print("Initializing YOLOv8x-seg model...")\n    model = YOLO(\'yolov8x-seg.pt\')\n\n    print("Starting model training...")\n    results = model.train(\n        data=data_yaml_path,\n        epochs=100,\n        patience=15,\n        imgsz=1024,\n        batch=8,\n        project=\'cell_segmentation_runs\',\n        name=\'yolov8x_1024px_100e\',\n        exist_ok=True,\n        fliplr=0.5,\n        flipud=0.5,\n        workers=8,\n        device=0,\n        val=True,\n    )\n\n    print("Loading best model checkpoint...")\n    best_model_path = results.save_dir / \'weights\' / \'best.pt\'\n    best_model = YOLO(best_model_path)\n\n    print("Performing inference on the validation set...")\n    seg_mask_list = []\n    \n    for i in range(len(val_dataset)):\n        img, _, _ = val_dataset[i]\n        preds = best_model.predict(\n            source=img,\n            imgsz=1024,\n            conf=0.25,\n            verbose=False,\n        )\n        \n        result = preds[0]\n        orig_H, orig_W = result.orig_shape\n        final_mask = np.zeros((orig_H, orig_W), dtype=np.uint16)\n\n        if result.masks is not None and result.boxes is not None and len(result.boxes.conf) > 0:\n            sorted_indices = torch.argsort(result.boxes.conf, descending=True)\n            masks_np = result.masks.data[sorted_indices].cpu().numpy()\n\n            for j, mask in enumerate(masks_np):\n                instance_id = j + 1\n                bool_mask = mask > 0.5\n                final_mask[bool_mask] = instance_id\n        \n        seg_mask_list.append(final_mask)\n        if (i + 1) % 10 == 0:\n            print(f"Processed {i + 1}/{len(val_dataset)} images.")\n\n    print("Inference complete.")\n    return seg_mask_list\n```'}, {'usage': {'prompt_tokens': 8119, 'completion_tokens': 152, 'total_tokens': 9668, 'latency': 14.340297677990748, 'cumulative_num_llm_calls': 4}, 'prompt_messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '# Introduction:\n You are a Computer Vision Expert writing programs for high-stakes scientific research. \nYou have written code to solve this task and now need to evaluate the output of the code execution.\nYou should determine if there were any bugs as well as report the empirical findings.\n\n# Task Description:\nYou are an experienced machine learning engineer who is helping scientists from different domains of science. Your task is to help\nscientists in domains that typically require many manual annotations. Specifically, you will be implementing programs that use \nmachine learning methods to train models on a set of data for a specific task.\n\nBENCHMARK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\nFirst, please read the instructions below which are specific to our benchmark setup. Note, these instructions can be found again in `/home/instructions.txt`.\n\n- **Task-specific instructions**: The instructions for the task are available in `./work/description.md`. Read this file to understand the problem setup for the task you are currently solving.\n\nTASK\n-----\nFor a given task, you will be provided with an initial program that you must optimize to maximize performance on the task.\n**Data setup and loading will be handled for you as input to the program, the task will describe the necessary output of your program.**\nThe focus of optimizations should be on machine learning engineering (i.e testing different models, hyperparameters, etc). Consider exploring a variety of model architectures and frameworks -- the data format or domain conventions should not constrain your choice of approach.\n\nADDITIONAL NOTES\n------\n\n- **Compute**: You have access to NVIDIA H100 NVL with the appropriate drivers installed.\n- **Total Runtime**: You have a maximum of 4hrs 0mins 0secs to attempt this task. You may iteratively develop your solution within this time frame, but bear in mind that program runtime also counts towards this limit.\n- **Total Steps**: You have a maximum of 500 steps to submit your solution.\n- You will be informed of the remaining time and steps at each step.\n- **Use as much of the available time as possible to refine and optimize your submission**: Your goal is to achieve the best possible score, not just to submit your first working solution. Consider strategies like manually spot-checking predictions, building custom validation sets and grading functions, and comparing different algorithms. You may keep working until the time limit expires -- as long as your best submission is stored at the designated location at the end of your run, it will be picked up for grading.\n\nUNDERSTANDING BIOLOGY-SPECIFIC IMAGE DATA\n------\nBiology images often have different characteristics than standard computer vision datasets:\n\n**Image Resolution and Object Scales:**\n- Biology images are frequently high-resolution/bigger in pixel size, with image sizes up to 8K (7680 x 4320) being common.\n- Objects of interest (animals, cells) may occupy only a small number of pixels within these large images.\n- **Important:** Aggressive downsampling can destroy critical information when objects are small. Carefully consider whether resizing is necessary and what resolution preserves the details needed for detection.\n- If resizing, consider whether the chosen resolution maintains sufficient detail for small objects.\n\nTASK INSTRUCTIONS\n------\n\n# Cell Segmentation\n\n## Dataset Description\n\nThe study of how cells grow, divide, and interact over time is a core problem in biology. A fundamental prerequisite for many downstream analyses—such as tracking, lineage inference, and morphology measurement—is cell segmentation: identifying which pixels belong to which individual cells in an image. The dataset consists of microscopy images of cells along with corresponding instance-level annotations. Each image may contain multiple cells, and each cell should be assigned a unique instance ID. Background pixels are labeled as 0. The data is exposed through a CellSegDataset abstraction, which provides access to images and instance masks and supports exporting the data into YOLO-compatible segmentation format.\n\n## Task Description\n\nYour goal is to implement a function called cell_segmentation that performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n\nYou must implement the following function:\n\n```python\ndef cell_segmentation(train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset):\n    """\n\tInput\n\t•\ttrain_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing training images and instance masks.\n\t•\tval_dataset: a CellSegDataset containing validation images (and possibly masks).\n\n    Returns:\n        seg_mask_list: list[np.ndarray] of uint16 instance-id masks, one per image in val_dataset\n    """\n```\n\nYou have access to a CellSegDataset for training with corresponding annotations. You must produce predictions on a validation CellSegDataset which you will be evaluated on.\n\nThe CellSegDataset class is provided below: \n\n```python\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """\n    General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index.\n\n    Returns:\n        image: H x W x 3 (RGB)\n        mask:  H x W (uint16 instance IDs) OR None if require_masks=False and missing\n        meta:  dict with filename info\n    """\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n\n        self.img_dir = self.data_dir\n        self.mask_dir = self.ann_dir\n\n        img_paths = sorted(self.img_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        if not img_paths:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No images found in {self.img_dir} (glob={image_glob})")\n\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.mask_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        if not mask_paths and require_masks:\n            raise RuntimeError(f"No masks found in {self.mask_dir} (glob={mask_glob})")\n\n        # Build index->path maps\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in img_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            img_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {}\n        for p in mask_paths:\n            idx = _extract_index(p.stem)\n            if idx is None:\n                continue\n            mask_by_idx[idx] = p\n\n        if not img_by_idx:\n            raise RuntimeError(\n                f"Found images in {self.img_dir} but none matched an index pattern (e.g., ...0000.tif)."\n            )\n\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                # Hard fail early so you don\'t discover this deep in training.\n                raise RuntimeError(\n                    f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}: image={img_p.name}. "\n                    f"Expected a mask with the same trailing index in {self.mask_dir} "\n                    f"(e.g., man_seg{idx:04d}.tif or mask{idx:04d}.tif)."\n                )\n\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n\n        # --- Load image ---\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n        # --- Load mask ---\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3: # Some GT masks can be saved as HxWx1\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path is not None else None,\n        }\n\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(self, yolo_dir: str | Path, *, class_id: int = 0, class_name: str = "cell", write_empty_labels: bool = True) -> str:\n        """\n        Generate Ultralytics YOLOv8-seg dataset for this dataset\'s split.\n\n        Writes:\n            yolo_dir/\n            images/<split>/*.png\n            labels/<split>/*.txt\n            data.yaml\n\n        Returns:\n            str path to yolo_dir/data.yaml\n        """\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        split = self.split\n\n        print(f"Building YOLO-seg dataset at {yolo_dir} (split={split})...")\n\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\n        num_written = 0\n\n        for idx, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            assert img_path.exists(), f"Missing image: {img_path}"\n\n            # ---------- IMAGE EXPORT (force 3-channel PNG) ----------\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n            out_img_name = f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / out_img_name\n\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n\n            # ---------- LABEL GENERATION ----------\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n\n            if mask_path is not None and Path(mask_path).exists():\n                iou, dice = mask_to_yolo_and_iou(\n                    mask_path=mask_path,\n                    txt_path=label_path,\n                    class_id=class_id,\n                )\n            else:\n                if write_empty_labels:\n                    label_path.touch()\n                else:\n                    continue\n\n            num_written += 1\n\n        # ---------- WRITE data.yaml ----------\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": "images/train",\n            "val": "images/val",\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            try:\n                with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                    existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            except Exception:\n                existing = {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n\n        self.yolo_dir = yolo_dir\n        self.yolo_yaml_path = str(data_yaml_path)\n\n        print(f"Done. Wrote {num_written} samples to split={split}.")\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\n    def get_yolo_dataset(self) -> str:\n        if not hasattr(self, "yolo_yaml_path"):\n            raise RuntimeError("YOLO dataset has not been generated yet. Call generate_yolo_dataset(...) first.")\n        return self.yolo_yaml_path\n\ndef collate_fn(batch):\n    imgs, masks, metas = zip(*batch)\n    return list(imgs), list(masks), list(metas)\n```\n\n# Implementation:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\nfrom ultralytics import YOLO\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport cv2\nimport yaml\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport re\nfrom typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# NOTE: The CellSegDataset class and its helpers are assumed to be provided by the environment.\n# They are included here to make the script self-contained and testable.\n\n\ndef _extract_index(stem: str) -> Optional[int]:\n    """Tries to extract a trailing integer index from a filename stem."""\n    m = re.search(r"\\d+$", stem)\n    if m:\n        return int(m.group())\n    return None\n\n\ndef _read_tif_any(p: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Read a TIF file, handling various compressions and bit depths."""\n    return cv2.imread(str(p), cv2.IMREAD_ANYDEPTH | cv2.IMREAD_ANYCOLOR)\n\n\ndef _as_rgb(img: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    """Ensure an image is 3-channel RGB."""\n    if img.ndim == 2:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 1:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_GRAY2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 4:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGRA2RGB)\n    if img.shape[2] == 3:\n        return cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_BGR2RGB)\n    raise ValueError(f"Unsupported image shape: {img.shape}")\n\n\ndef mask_to_yolo(mask_path: Path, txt_path: Path, class_id: int = 0):\n    """Converts a single instance mask to YOLO segmentation format and saves it."""\n    mask = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n    if mask.ndim == 3:\n        mask = mask[..., 0]\n\n    h, w = mask.shape\n    segments = []\n    instance_ids = np.unique(mask)\n    instance_ids = instance_ids[instance_ids != 0]\n\n    for inst_id in instance_ids:\n        binary_mask = (mask == inst_id).astype(np.uint8)\n        contours, _ = cv2.findContours(\n            binary_mask, cv2.RETR_EXTERNAL, cv2.CHAIN_APPROX_SIMPLE\n        )\n\n        segment = []\n        if contours:\n            largest_contour = max(contours, key=cv2.contourArea)\n            if cv2.contourArea(largest_contour) > 10:\n                largest_contour = largest_contour.flatten().tolist()\n                segment = [\n                    coord / w if i % 2 == 0 else coord / h\n                    for i, coord in enumerate(largest_contour)\n                ]\n\n        if segment:\n            segments.append(segment)\n\n    with open(txt_path, "w") as f:\n        for segment in segments:\n            line = f"{class_id} " + " ".join(map(str, segment))\n            f.write(line + "\\n")\n\n\nclass CellSegDataset(Dataset):\n    """General-purpose segmentation dataset that pairs images and masks by numeric index."""\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data_dir,\n        ann_dir,\n        split: str = "train",\n        require_masks: bool = True,\n        image_glob: str = "*.tif",\n        mask_glob: str = "*.tif",\n    ):\n        self.data_dir = Path(data_dir)\n        self.ann_dir = Path(ann_dir)\n        self.split = split\n        self.require_masks = require_masks\n        img_paths = sorted(self.data_dir.glob(image_glob))\n        mask_paths = sorted(self.ann_dir.glob(mask_glob))\n        img_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {\n            p: _extract_index(p.stem)\n            for p in img_paths\n            if _extract_index(p.stem) is not None\n        }\n        mask_by_idx: Dict[int, Path] = {\n            p: _extract_index(p.stem)\n            for p in mask_paths\n            if _extract_index(p.stem) is not None\n        }\n        img_by_idx = {v: k for k, v in img_by_idx.items()}\n        mask_by_idx = {v: k for k, v in mask_by_idx.items()}\n        self.pairs: List[Tuple[int, Path, Optional[Path]]] = []\n        for idx in sorted(img_by_idx.keys()):\n            img_p = img_by_idx[idx]\n            m_p = mask_by_idx.get(idx, None)\n            if self.require_masks and m_p is None:\n                raise RuntimeError(f"Missing mask for image idx={idx}")\n            self.pairs.append((idx, img_p, m_p))\n        if not self.pairs:\n            raise RuntimeError("No (image, mask) pairs could be formed.")\n\n    def __len__(self):\n        return len(self.pairs)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, i):\n        idx, img_path, mask_path = self.pairs[i]\n        img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n        img = _as_rgb(img)\n        mask = None\n        if mask_path is not None:\n            m = _read_tif_any(mask_path)\n            if m.ndim == 3:\n                m = m[..., 0]\n            mask = m.astype(np.uint16)\n        meta = {\n            "index": idx,\n            "image_id": img_path.stem,\n            "image_path": str(img_path),\n            "mask_path": str(mask_path) if mask_path else None,\n        }\n        return img, mask, meta\n\n    def generate_yolo_dataset(\n        self,\n        yolo_dir: str | Path,\n        *,\n        class_id: int = 0,\n        class_name: str = "cell",\n        write_empty_labels: bool = True,\n    ) -> str:\n        yolo_dir = Path(yolo_dir)\n        yolo_image_dir = yolo_dir / "images" / self.split\n        yolo_label_dir = yolo_dir / "labels" / self.split\n        yolo_image_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        yolo_label_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        for _, img_path, mask_path in self.pairs:\n            img = _read_tif_any(img_path)\n            img = _as_rgb(img)\n            if img.dtype != np.uint8:\n                if img.dtype == np.uint16:\n                    img = (img / 256).astype(np.uint8)\n                else:\n                    img = np.clip(img, 0, 255).astype(np.uint8)\n            out_img_path = yolo_image_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.png"\n            if not out_img_path.exists():\n                cv2.imwrite(str(out_img_path), cv2.cvtColor(img, cv2.COLOR_RGB2BGR))\n            label_path = yolo_label_dir / f"{img_path.stem}.txt"\n            if mask_path and mask_path.exists():\n                mask_to_yolo(\n                    mask_path=mask_path, txt_path=label_path, class_id=class_id\n                )\n            elif write_empty_labels:\n                label_path.touch()\n        data_yaml_path = yolo_dir / "data.yaml"\n        yaml_config = {\n            "path": str(yolo_dir.resolve()),\n            "train": "images/train",\n            "val": "images/val",\n            "names": {class_id: class_name},\n        }\n        if data_yaml_path.exists():\n            with open(data_yaml_path, "r") as f:\n                existing = yaml.safe_load(f) or {}\n            existing.update(yaml_config)\n            yaml_config = existing\n        with open(data_yaml_path, "w") as f:\n            yaml.safe_dump(yaml_config, f, sort_keys=False)\n        return str(data_yaml_path)\n\n\ndef cell_segmentation(\n    train_dataset: CellSegDataset, val_dataset: CellSegDataset\n) -> List[np.ndarray]:\n    """\n    Performs instance segmentation on a validation dataset of cell images.\n    """\n    yolo_dir = Path("./yolo_cell_dataset")\n    if yolo_dir.exists():\n        shutil.rmtree(yolo_dir)\n    yolo_dir.mkdir(parents=True)\n\n    print("Generating YOLO formatted data...")\n    data_yaml_path = train_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(\n        yolo_dir=yolo_dir, split="train"\n    )\n    _ = val_dataset.generate_yolo_dataset(yolo_dir=yolo_dir, split="val")\n\n    print("Initializing YOLOv8x-seg model...")\n    model = YOLO("yolov8x-seg.pt")\n\n    print("Starting model training...")\n    results = model.train(\n        data=data_yaml_path,\n        epochs=100,\n        patience=15,\n        imgsz=1024,\n        batch=8,\n        project="cell_segmentation_runs",\n        name="yolov8x_1024px_100e",\n        exist_ok=True,\n        fliplr=0.5,\n        flipud=0.5,\n        workers=8,\n        device=0,\n        val=True,\n    )\n\n    print("Loading best model checkpoint...")\n    best_model_path = results.save_dir / "weights" / "best.pt"\n    best_model = YOLO(best_model_path)\n\n    print("Performing inference on the validation set...")\n    seg_mask_list = []\n\n    for i in range(len(val_dataset)):\n        img, _, _ = val_dataset[i]\n        preds = best_model.predict(\n            source=img,\n            imgsz=1024,\n            conf=0.25,\n            verbose=False,\n        )\n\n        result = preds[0]\n        orig_H, orig_W = result.orig_shape\n        final_mask = np.zeros((orig_H, orig_W), dtype=np.uint16)\n\n        if (\n            result.masks is not None\n            and result.boxes is not None\n            and len(result.boxes.conf) > 0\n        ):\n            sorted_indices = torch.argsort(result.boxes.conf, descending=True)\n            masks_np = result.masks.data[sorted_indices].cpu().numpy()\n\n            for j, mask in enumerate(masks_np):\n                instance_id = j + 1\n                bool_mask = mask > 0.5\n                final_mask[bool_mask] = instance_id\n\n        seg_mask_list.append(final_mask)\n        if (i + 1) % 10 == 0:\n            print(f"Processed {i + 1}/{len(val_dataset)} images.")\n\n    print("Inference complete.")\n    return seg_mask_list\n\n```\n\n# Execution output:\n```\n✅ Added /work to sys.path\nWorking dir: /\n✅ Added /work to sys.path\nWork